0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","it-act",{"law":{"id":"it-act","title":"Information Technology Act, 2000","short":"IT Act","year":"2000 · amended through the notified text","kind":"Act","status":"current","summary":"The central technology-specific statute: electronic governance, security, cyber offences, state powers, intermediary liability, and institutional functions.","url":"https://www.meity.gov.in/static/uploads/2024/06/2bf1f0e9f04e6fb4f8fef35e82c42aa5.pdf","caution":"Section 66A was struck down in Shreya Singhal (2015). Old compilations may still print it. The BNS did not repeal the IT Act.","topics":["1.6","1.4","2.3"],"sections":[{"number":"1","title":"Extent and extraterritorial application","text":"Read with section 75. The Act extends to the whole of India and, in specified circumstances, to offences or contraventions outside India involving a computer, system, or network located in India. Extraterritorial reach is not unlimited enforcement jurisdiction."},{"number":"2","title":"Definitions","text":"Defines terms including computer, computer resource, communication device, data, electronic record, intermediary, and secure electronic record. Check the exact clause and amendments before applying a definition; everyday meanings may be narrower or broader."},{"number":"4–5","title":"Legal recognition of electronic records and signatures","text":"Provide legal-recognition rules for electronic records and electronic authentication, subject to exclusions and prescribed requirements. Recognition is not a guarantee of authenticity, execution, or admissibility in every dispute."},{"number":"7–10A","title":"Retention, government records, and electronic contracts","text":"Address retention, use in government processes, signature-related rules, and contract formation through electronic means. Other statutes and excluded document categories still matter."},{"number":"43","title":"Damage to computer systems","text":"Addresses specified acts without permission, including unauthorized access, downloading, introducing contaminants, damage, disruption, denial of access, and assistance, with compensation under the provision. Identify the precise clause and the affected system."},{"number":"43A","title":"Sensitive personal data security","text":"Concerns compensation for a body corporate’s negligent failure to maintain reasonable security practices for sensitive personal data it possesses, deals with, or handles, where wrongful loss or gain results. Read with the 2011 SPDI Rules and the DPDP commencement framework."},{"number":"65","title":"Tampering with computer source documents","text":"Addresses knowing or intentional concealment, destruction, or alteration of computer source code required to be kept or produced by law. This is not the old Evidence Act section 65 on secondary evidence."},{"number":"66","title":"Computer-related offences","text":"Criminalizes dishonest or fraudulent commission of acts referred to in section 43. It is not a general offence of every unauthorized use; the underlying act and required mental element must be established."},{"number":"66A","title":"Offensive messages — struck down","text":"The Supreme Court struck down section 66A in Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (2015). Do not present it as a valid current charge merely because it appears in an old bare Act or case template."},{"number":"66B–66D","title":"Stolen resources, identity theft, and personation","text":"66B concerns dishonest receipt or retention of stolen computer resources or communication devices; 66C addresses fraudulent or dishonest use of specified identification features; 66D addresses cheating by personation using a computer resource. Distinguish the ingredients."},{"number":"66E–66F","title":"Privacy and terrorism","text":"66E addresses specified intentional private-image capture or dissemination without consent. 66F addresses cyber terrorism under defined intent, means, and consequences; not every intrusion or politically motivated post satisfies it."},{"number":"67–67C","title":"Electronic publication and preservation offences","text":"67 concerns specified obscene electronic material; 67A sexually explicit material; 67B specified child sexual exploitation material; 67C failure by a person in charge to preserve specified information. Apply exact definitions and sensitive-handling safeguards."},{"number":"69","title":"Interception, monitoring, and decryption","text":"Authorizes specified action under the statutory grounds and procedure, including the 2009 Rules. It is distinct from a section 91 production order, a search, and a section 69A blocking direction."},{"number":"69A–69B","title":"Blocking and traffic data","text":"69A provides a blocking framework for specified sovereign, security, public-order, and related interests, with procedural safeguards. 69B concerns authorized collection of specified traffic data for cybersecurity functions. Neither is a blanket investigative permission."},{"number":"70–70B","title":"Protected systems and national institutions","text":"Section 70 addresses notified protected systems. Sections 70A and 70B concern critical information infrastructure protection and CERT-In functions. Verify current institutional notifications and the exact scope of each role."},{"number":"72–72A","title":"Confidentiality and personal-data disclosure","text":"Address specified breaches of confidentiality and disclosure of personal information obtained under contractual or statutory powers. Consent, lawful purpose, and the conditions of the provision must be examined."},{"number":"75","title":"Extraterritorial operation","text":"Extends specified application to conduct outside India involving a computer, computer system, or network located in India. Coordinate with procedural law and international cooperation for overseas evidence."},{"number":"79","title":"Conditional intermediary safe harbour","text":"Protects qualifying intermediaries in specified circumstances, subject to subsections (2) and (3), applicable Rules, and judicial interpretation. It is not immunity for every act or a substitute for due diligence."},{"number":"80–81","title":"Police powers and overriding effect","text":"Section 80 provides specified police powers for offences under the Act. Section 81 gives overriding effect subject to its wording. Neither eliminates constitutional or procedural safeguards."},{"number":"87","title":"Power to make rules","text":"Provides the delegated authority under which multiple rule sets are framed. Always identify the particular rule, clause, notification, and amendment rather than citing only “the IT Rules”."},{"number":"94","title":"Short title, extent, and commencement","text":"Contains the Act’s citation, extent, and commencement provisions. For a general production order concerning electronic records, consult BNSS section 94 (formerly CrPC section 91), not a supposed IT Act section 91 production power. The IT Act’s section 91 concerns amendment of the Indian Penal Code."}]}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
